text:
**Refined prompt:** Review the proposed `UserService.update()` password-hashing hunk as a staff-level reviewer on this RealWorld API — both layers (local correctness *and* design integrity: invariants, cross-consumer impact, project conventions), covering (a) per-finding severity · file:line · failure scenario · fix and (b) a ship/no-ship verdict for pushing. · Assumptions: the diff is not yet applied — `src/user/user.service.ts:74-81` on disk is still the pre-change version, so I reviewed the hunk as proposed against the surrounding code.

I could not run `tsc` (no `node_modules` in this checkout), so the compile-error claims below are read off the declared types, not a build.

---

### 🔴 Blocker · `src/user/user.service.ts` (new line, `if (dto.password)`) — `password` is not on `UpdateUserDto`; this does not compile

`src/user/dto/update-user.dto.ts:1-6` declares exactly `username`, `email`, `bio`, `image`. `dto.password` is TS2339 (*Property 'password' does not exist*), which fires even with `"noImplicitAny": false` (`tsconfig.json:5`). Both the guard and the `argon2.hash(dto.password)` argument error.

**Fix:** add `readonly password?: string;` to `UpdateUserDto` — necessary but not sufficient, see the next two findings.

### 🔴 Blocker · `src/user/user.service.ts` (new line) — missing `await`; a `Promise` is written to the password column

`argon2.hash()` returns `Promise<string>`. `updated.password = argon2.hash(...)` assigns the pending promise. Today that is *also* a compile error (TS2322, `Promise<string>` → `string`), so it can't ship in this exact form — but the obvious way to clear Blocker 1 is to type the new DTO field loosely, and then this one goes quiet and ships.

**Failure scenario:** `PUT /api/user` `{"user":{"password":"newpassword"}}` → column gets `[object Promise]` → next login hits `argon2.verify(user.password, password)` at `user.service.ts:31` with a malformed hash → argon2 throws → 500 from `POST /users/login`, and the account is unrecoverable. That is `LESSONS.md` #88 reproduced in a new form: the update path still doesn't produce a hash.

**Fix:** `updated.password = await argon2.hash(dto.password);`

### 🔴 Blocker · `src/user/user.service.ts:79` — the raw-body merge is untouched, and it's the other half of this bug

`CURRENT_FOCUS.md` scopes this work as two things: *hash on update* **and** *stop the raw body being merged into the entity*. The diff does the first and leaves the second, while adding a branch that depends on it.

`PUT /user` (`user.controller.ts:26-29`) carries no `@UsePipes(new ValidationPipe())` — unlike `POST /users` (`:31`) and `POST /users/login` (`:42`) — and `main.ts:5-21` registers no global pipe. So `@Body('user')` is the raw client object and `UpdateUserDto` erases at runtime (`LESSONS.md` #112).

**Who issues the bad input:** any authenticated client. Three concrete failures:

1. `{"user":{"id":42,"bio":"x"}}` → `Object.assign` overwrites the loaded entity's PK → `save()` at `:80` writes row 42. One user edits another's record.
2. `{"user":{"email":"victim@example.com"}}` → succeeds. The uniqueness query exists only in `create()` (`:42-47`), there's no unique index, and `findOne({email})` at `:26` then returns whichever duplicate row MySQL yields first.
3. `{"user":{"password":""}}` → `Object.assign` runs **before** the guard and plants `""` on the entity; `if (dto.password)` is falsy so the hash is skipped; `save()` writes `password = ''`. Account bricked by a falsy value the guard was supposed to catch. A truthiness check is the wrong test for presence.

⚠️ **The pipe is not the fix here.** `src/shared/pipes/validation.pipe.ts:23` returns `value` — the original body — not the `plainToClass`'d `object` from `:18`. It validates and hands back the raw payload unfiltered. Adding `@UsePipes` to this handler would strip nothing.

**Fix:** copy allowed fields by name in the service (see patch below).

### 🟠 High · `src/user/user.controller.ts:26-29` — the diff makes `PUT /user` return the argon2 hash to the client

Pre-change, `:76 delete toUpdate.password` removed the key and nothing restored it, so `password` was absent from the JSON. Post-change, on a password update `updated.password` is set, `save()` returns that same mutated object, and the controller returns it with no projection.

**Failure scenario:** `PUT /api/user` `{"user":{"password":"s3cret"}}` → `200 {"id":1,"username":"jake","email":"...","password":"$argon2i$v=19$..."}`. A credential secret in a response body — proxy-logged, cached, in every client's network tab.

This lands because `update()` returns a bare `UserEntity` instead of the envelope (`LESSONS.md` #57 — the one user handler that skips `buildUserRO()`). `buildUserRO()` at `:116-127` projects exactly `id/username/email/bio/token/image` and would have dropped the hash for free, plus it restores the `token` and `{user}` wrapper that RealWorld frontends read after a profile save. `userService.update()` has exactly one caller (`user.controller.ts:28`), so changing the return type is safe.

### 🟡 Medium · `src/user/user.service.ts:74-81` — no validation on the update path

`@IsEmail()` (`user.entity.ts:16`) is only enforced by the explicit `validate()` call in `create()` (`:62`); `update()` never validates. `PUT /user` with `{"email":"not-an-email"}` persists it. Pre-existing, not introduced here — but a password-change path is the moment to close it, and the fix is the same allowlist.

### 🔵 Comment — don't "fix" this with `@BeforeUpdate()`

Adding it beside `@BeforeInsert()` at `user.entity.ts:28` looks tidy and is wrong: on every non-password update `password` is `undefined` (deleted at `:76`) so the hook calls `argon2.hash(undefined)` and throws, and where it is set it would re-hash an already-hashed value. The diff's instinct — hash explicitly in the service — is the right one. Only the implementation is off.

---

### Suggested replacement for `:74-81`

```ts
  async update(id: number, dto: UpdateUserDto): Promise<UserRO> {
    const toUpdate = await this.userRepository.findOne(id);
    if (!toUpdate) {
      throw new HttpException({errors: {User: ' not found'}}, 401);
    }

    // Copy allowed fields by name — the DTO type strips nothing at runtime
    // and PUT /user has no whitelisting pipe. See LESSONS.md #112.
    if (dto.username !== undefined) toUpdate.username = dto.username;
    if (dto.email !== undefined) toUpdate.email = dto.email;
    if (dto.bio !== undefined) toUpdate.bio = dto.bio;
    if (dto.image !== undefined) toUpdate.image = dto.image;
    if (dto.password !== undefined) {
      // @BeforeInsert() does not run on update — hash explicitly.
      toUpdate.password = await argon2.hash(dto.password);
    }

    const saved = await this.userRepository.save(toUpdate);
    return this.buildUserRO(saved);
  }
```

With the allowlist, `delete toUpdate.password` / `delete toUpdate.favorites` become unnecessary — nothing untrusted reaches the entity, unchanged columns are omitted from the UPDATE anyway, and `favorites` is a non-eager relation so it was already `undefined`. Add `readonly password?: string;` to `UpdateUserDto` and widen the other four to optional, since a partial update is the normal case.

---

## Verdict: **NEEDS CHANGES** — do not push

The hunk does not compile as written, and if the compile errors are cleared the lazy way it writes a `Promise` into the password column and locks users out — the same class of failure the change was meant to fix. It also newly leaks the argon2 hash in the `PUT /user` response, and leaves the raw-body merge that lets one user write another user's row.

**Parts confirmed addressed:** (a) review of the change — 4 actionable findings + 1 design comment, each with severity, `file:line`, failure scenario and fix; (b) push-readiness verdict — stated above. **Not covered:** I did not run the build or any test (no `node_modules`), and I did not read the `article` module beyond confirming `ArticleService.update()` shares the same `Object.assign` shape.
